8. Five levels of Dhaval Obscurity

Dhaval Obscurity has exactly five severity levels.

Initial application

When an eligible racer is first successfully affected:

Level 1

Never start at 0 while the debuff is visibly active.

Escalation

While that racer is currently under Dhaval Obscurity:

every genuine collision with a road hazard increases Obscurity by exactly one level.

Example:

first affected → Level 1
hits one hazard → Level 2
hits another hazard → Level 3
hits another hazard → Level 4
hits another hazard → Level 5

Cap at:

Level 5

Further hazard collisions must not exceed 5.

What qualifies as a collision

Escalate only after the collision system has determined that the racer genuinely contacted the hazard.

Do not escalate for:

* a near miss
* a Perfect Dodge
* a hazard passing nearby
* an already-spent hazard
* multiple checks of the same collision in one simulation step
* a racer that is not currently Dhaval-obscured
* a target for whom the hazard interaction was impossible because they were physically elsewhere

A shield absorbing the gameplay consequence is still a real physical hazard contact. If the collision was genuinely accepted, it may advance Dhaval Obscurity even if shield durability prevented the usual debuff/wreck.

However, if the racer’s own active ultimate destroys the hazard before it can actually hit them, that is not an Obscurity-escalating collision.

Hook escalation into the authoritative accepted-collision paths, not into visual overlap detection.

Cover both:

* player hazard handling in mechanics.js
* rival hazard handling in race.js

and every applicable G.traps hazard type.

Reset behavior

A new, unrelated Dhaval Obscurity episode starts again at Level 1.

Reset the Dhaval Obscurity level when the debuff is genuinely cleared, including:

* Cleansed
* wreck/debuff cleanup
* race reset
* finish cleanup where appropriate
* complete natural expiry

Do not leave Level 5 silently stored so the next fresh application begins at Level 5.

