11. Exact visual progression from Level 1 to Level 5

Severity must increase clearly and monotonically.

The five levels should differ through a combination of:

* number of circles
* average radius
* opacity
* overlap
* movement density
* amount of screen coverage
* frequency of new pop-ins
* brightness

Target the following visual read:

Level 1 — Light obstruction

The player can still see most of the road.

Use:

* relatively few circles
* significant empty gaps
* lower opacity
* moderate radii
* slower/cleaner movement

Approximate perceived obstruction: 15–25%.

It should be obvious that something is affecting the driver but should not dominate the whole screen.

Level 2 — Noticeable

More circles and more overlap.

Approximate perceived obstruction: 30–40%.

Important racing information is still visible through frequent openings.

Level 3 — Heavy

The lights are now a serious visibility problem.

Approximate perceived obstruction: 50–60%.

Large circles should regularly cross the player’s primary forward view.

Level 4 — Severe

The road is difficult to follow.

Approximate perceived obstruction: 70–80%.

Use many large overlapping lights with only intermittent openings.

Level 5 — Almost blinded

This is the maximum state.

The racer should barely be able to see the road.

Approximate perceived obstruction: 85–95% across time.

Use huge overlapping white/purple/red/green lights with high density.

Do not simply replace the entire viewport with one opaque rectangle. There should still be tiny moving openings and visual evidence that a race is continuing underneath.

The result should feel like the player’s vision has become almost unusable because of the light storm, not because the renderer stopped drawing the game.

